1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,663
Welcome to Atomic Education.

2
00:00:02,723 --> 00:00:09,910
This lesson was written by the Atomic teaching team to help you learn Cambridge Lower Secondary Science with confidence.

3
00:00:09,910 --> 00:00:10,623
Let's begin!

4
00:00:11,682 --> 00:00:13,227
Welcome to Unit 11: Sound.

5
00:00:14,286 --> 00:00:15,949
Close your eyes and listen.

6
00:00:15,949 --> 00:00:21,889
A car engine, a bird singing, people talking: some sounds are loud, some soft, some high, some low.

7
00:00:21,889 --> 00:00:25,394
Where do sounds come from, and how do they reach your ears?

8
00:00:26,453 --> 00:00:32,334
All sounds come from vibrating, or oscillating, objects. A plucked guitar string, a drum membrane,

9
00:00:32,334 --> 00:00:36,670
the reed of a saxophone, your vocal cords and a speaker cone all vibrate.

10
00:00:37,729 --> 00:00:42,362
A speaker cone moving forward squeezes air particles together: a compression.

11
00:00:42,362 --> 00:00:45,392
Moving back, it spreads them apart: a rarefaction.

12
00:00:45,392 --> 00:00:50,856
These travel outwards as a sound wave, passing energy from particle to particle to your ear.

13
00:00:51,916 --> 00:00:54,292
Sound needs a medium to travel through.

14
00:00:54,292 --> 00:01:00,054
The closer the particles, the faster sound travels: fastest in solids, then liquids, then gases.

15
00:01:00,054 --> 00:01:04,924
In a vacuum there are no particles, so sound can't travel at all. Space is silent!

16
00:01:05,984 --> 00:01:11,567
An electric bell rings inside a sealed glass jar. A pump slowly removes the air from the jar.

17
00:01:11,567 --> 00:01:15,428
Predict: what will happen to the sound we hear? Pause and decide.

18
00:01:16,488 --> 00:01:20,646
Watch. As the air is pumped out, the ringing gets quieter and quieter.

19
00:01:21,705 --> 00:01:25,388
With almost all the air removed, we can hardly hear anything,

20
00:01:25,388 --> 00:01:28,595
although we can still see the hammer hitting the bell.

21
00:01:29,655 --> 00:01:32,446
Explain what you saw. The bell still vibrates,

22
00:01:32,446 --> 00:01:37,495
but sound needs particles to carry the vibrations to the glass and then to our ears.

23
00:01:37,495 --> 00:01:43,198
As the air is removed, there are fewer particles to pass on the vibrations, so the sound fades.

24
00:01:43,198 --> 00:01:47,950
In a vacuum, sound cannot travel at all. Pause and explain it in your own words.

25
00:01:49,009 --> 00:01:54,236
The speed of sound depends on the material it travels through. These are typical values.

26
00:01:55,296 --> 00:01:59,810
In air at 20 degrees Celsius, sound travels at about 343 metres per second.

27
00:01:59,810 --> 00:02:02,899
In water it is about 1480, and in steel nearly 6000.

28
00:02:03,958 --> 00:02:07,938
Pause and decide: why is sound fastest in steel and slowest in air?
